WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.300
- So you had to do a lot of your scenes

00:00:02.300 --> 00:00:04.016
with an animated character,

00:00:04.016 --> 00:00:04.960
so how does that work since,

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:06.460
you know, that's not real.

00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:07.601
- Yeah.

00:00:07.601 --> 00:00:08.434
- Yeah.

00:00:08.434 --> 00:00:10.115
- A vivid imagination.

00:00:10.115 --> 00:00:10.948
- [Kelly] Well done.

00:00:10.948 --> 00:00:12.540
- You're staring into, you know,

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:15.280
middle space of nothing mostly.

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:18.040
Basically trying not to lose
your mind on a daily basis.

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:19.860
- Yeah, 'cause that would be really hard.

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:21.830
- Yeah you know, you get used to it.

00:00:21.830 --> 00:00:22.663
It's like okay great,

00:00:22.663 --> 00:00:25.020
I don't have to wait on
other actors to show up.

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:27.240
I can create my own actor
in front of my face.

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:29.200
- So he's the diva on set, okay I gotcha.

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:30.391
(audience laughs and
claps) No, just kidding.

00:00:30.391 --> 00:00:31.224
I'm just kidding. (laughs)

00:00:31.224 --> 00:00:32.057
- It's crazy that stuff.

00:00:32.057 --> 00:00:32.890
It's amazing.

00:00:32.890 --> 00:00:34.170
It's modern pantomime.

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:35.090
- Yeah, it is right?

00:00:35.090 --> 00:00:36.840
And also the audience can tell

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:38.860
if you were looking at
something too far away,

00:00:38.860 --> 00:00:39.851
if you're --

00:00:39.851 --> 00:00:40.684
- That's what I mean, your eyeline,

00:00:40.684 --> 00:00:42.410
- If Sonic's here, there's a
triangulation of your eyes,

00:00:42.410 --> 00:00:44.086
you have to like train your eyes --

00:00:44.086 --> 00:00:45.820
- I literally had to do
music video for Ugly Dolls

00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:47.290
and they were like,
here are the characters

00:00:47.290 --> 00:00:49.156
and I felt, I was like,

00:00:49.156 --> 00:00:50.410
I'm gonna suck at this

00:00:50.410 --> 00:00:51.750
and you did a whole movie like that,

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:52.583
I can't imagine.

00:00:52.583 --> 00:00:55.668
- Well we worked with this
stuffed animal sitting there.

00:00:55.668 --> 00:00:59.410
- Yeah, it's an early
stuffed version of Sonic

00:00:59.410 --> 00:01:02.697
and his eyes kinda look like
Jim's on the screen over there.

00:01:02.697 --> 00:01:03.530
(laughs)

00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:05.320
This kinda glued on,

00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:07.563
kind of an early, crude,

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:10.978
plushy Sonic.

00:01:10.978 --> 00:01:11.811
- That's so creepy.

00:01:11.811 --> 00:01:13.250
So something we both have in common

00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:14.910
in speaking of movies and
roles you always take,

00:01:14.910 --> 00:01:17.230
I said this backstage
but we both love singing

00:01:17.230 --> 00:01:19.435
and you're so good at it,

00:01:19.435 --> 00:01:20.268
you're so good!

00:01:20.268 --> 00:01:21.101
- Oh well thank you.

00:01:21.101 --> 00:01:21.980
(audience cheers and claps)

00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:22.813
Yeah!

00:01:22.813 --> 00:01:24.140
Like I love actors that come out

00:01:24.140 --> 00:01:25.900
and they just can do everything.

00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:27.420
They're singing, they're
dancing, they're acting,

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:28.480
it's so cool.

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:29.821
- Ya have to.

00:01:29.821 --> 00:01:30.663
Have to do it.

00:01:30.663 --> 00:01:31.496
- Yeah you just have to.

00:01:31.496 --> 00:01:32.329
- Ya gotta do it.

00:01:32.329 --> 00:01:33.162
It's like the old studio days,

00:01:33.162 --> 00:01:34.695
you gotta sing, dance, all of it.

00:01:34.695 --> 00:01:36.249
- I just do the one.

00:01:36.249 --> 00:01:37.082
- You know what it is?

00:01:37.082 --> 00:01:38.760
Maybe Jim feels the same way,

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:40.750
I was always just a mimic.

00:01:40.750 --> 00:01:43.640
I could always hear rhythms of dialogue,

00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:45.440
I could hear rhythms of music

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:46.273
and I could hear the pitch

00:01:46.273 --> 00:01:48.710
and I would just emulate
singers that I liked.

00:01:48.710 --> 00:01:49.930
- Yes.

00:01:49.930 --> 00:01:51.155
- I could sing, it just was,

00:01:51.155 --> 00:01:53.249
that's kinda where it came from.

00:01:53.249 --> 00:01:54.082
- Did you grow up in choir?

00:01:54.082 --> 00:01:55.919
- I wasn't allowed to go outside so,

00:01:55.919 --> 00:02:00.450
a lot of "Boy in the Plastic
Bubble" tear going on,

00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:01.868
so I used to,

00:02:01.868 --> 00:02:04.620
imitate Johnny Mathis
and people like that.

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:06.840
I used to come up singing impressions.

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:10.450
So yeah, we can basically
fake our way through anything.

00:02:10.450 --> 00:02:11.283
- Yeah.

00:02:11.283 --> 00:02:12.116
- That's it.

00:02:12.116 --> 00:02:12.949
- So can women,

00:02:12.949 --> 00:02:13.930
da doomp, chh!

00:02:13.930 --> 00:02:14.895
(audience cheers and claps)

00:02:14.895 --> 00:02:16.747
- Ah, come on!

00:02:16.747 --> 00:02:17.580
- Wait, what?!

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:19.180
- [Jim] Come on!

00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:20.013
- That happens?

00:02:20.013 --> 00:02:21.000
- I'm just kidding, it's daytime!

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:23.589
- Yeah but why would you
fake it four or five times?

00:02:23.589 --> 00:02:25.672
(laughs)

00:02:26.877 --> 00:02:28.270
Oh I'm sorry,

00:02:28.270 --> 00:02:29.440
did I say too much?

00:02:31.052 --> 00:02:32.929
- I'm amazing!

00:02:32.929 --> 00:02:33.762
So wait --

00:02:33.762 --> 00:02:37.033
- You coulda stopped at three.
- This is.

